#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139353} :activity/id #uuid "753f3c2c-963f-5bf9-82db-0290639a9a4b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049281 :topic/id #uuid "e9994744-cb83-57d9-8154-ea5ac4c05cc8" :topic/math-academy-id 2274} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139353 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139717 :tutorial/content "A good example of a direct variation model is the distance an object travels when moving at a constant rate. In this case, the corresponding direct variation equation is \n\n$$\nd=vt,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $d$ is the total distance traveled by the object,\n\n- $t$ is the total time that the object has been traveling, and,\n\n- $v$ is the constant of proportionality, called the **speed** of the object.\n\nFor instance, if an object travels a distance of $24\\,\\textrm m$ in $4$ seconds, then we can substitute $d=24\\,\\textrm m$ and $t=4\\,\\textrm s$ into the above equation and solve for $v,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24 & =v\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\frac{24}{4} & =v \\\\ 6 & =v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation that relates $d$ and $t$ is\n\n$$\nd=6t.\n$$\n\n \n\nOne thing to note is that the constant of proportionality $v = 6$ also has units. So let's work out what these units are.\n\nSince we have\n\n$$\nd = vt,\n$$\n\nit follows that\n\n$$\nv = \\dfrac{d}{t}.\n$$\n\nSo, the units of $v$ are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm m}{\\textrm s} = \\textrm m / \\textrm s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the constant of proportionality (including its unit) is $v = 6\\, \\textrm m / \\textrm s.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "dcda3158-97b6-5a4a-b062-3803a3db7b26" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8753 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "577c6468-9f0e-55fb-84fc-17fcc4e6bca8" :step/math-academy-id 196782 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139354}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139354 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138430 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138166 :question/id #uuid "f01f91de-9010-5429-ba83-faaf90c866d7" :question/math-academy-id "e-14086"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a35a2fee-a1a5-59aa-9390-f73d2898320c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049141}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138736} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499543} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541112}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Constant of Variation"} :step/id #uuid "fd7ad040-afed-5ba6-ad72-f3ed0909fc54" :step/math-academy-id 196783 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139355}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139355 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138431 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138168 :question/id #uuid "f1408bad-ae5c-55fb-b78e-60ee0d8a1ca0" :question/math-academy-id "e-14088"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7df32abc-07bd-5213-a838-50e893ff5759" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138664} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499547}] :knowledge-point/title "Modeling With Direct Variation"} :step/id #uuid "d2efc257-0a27-570d-a356-98d12f63e6c2" :step/math-academy-id 196786 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139356}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139356 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139719 :tutorial/content "When modeling proportional relationships, we sometimes find that a quantity $y$ varies directly with the *square* of a second quantity $x.$ In such cases, we write\n\n$$\ny = kx^2.\n$$\n\nFor example, consider a circle of radius $r,$ measured in centimeters.\n\n![](images/fa/fad3151a754d96d3e4ad97fa812ce7087601cebe7c4ab00f7b3c8cd603b140c0.png)\n\nIt can be shown that the area $A,$ measured in square meters, of a circle varies directly with the *square* of the radius $r.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\nA = k r^2.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c5a8d87f-edfb-5e37-ad6d-6cafde48e4ef" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8755 :tutorial/title "Variations With Second Powers"} :step/id #uuid "80f09102-b1f8-5a4e-8df1-f285f0944581" :step/math-academy-id 196789 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139357}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139357 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138432 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138171 :question/id #uuid "201cba9a-1d9e-5bf3-b312-d84fb3d59407" :question/math-academy-id "e-14091"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01db3ebe-66ec-5340-8e29-c99fb149f5da" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138739} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499473} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499532}] :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Direct Variations With Second Powers"} :step/id #uuid "458a0b34-716e-5233-8ee6-52e6a9afd6a3" :step/math-academy-id 196790}] :activity/title "Modeling with Direct Variation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186138600}]}
